---
/*
 * SleepingGiantMark - Sibley Creek's canonical brand mark.
 *
 * A one-continuous-line stylized silhouette of the Sleeping Giant, the
 * landform on the Sibley Peninsula in Lake Superior (Sleeping Giant
 * Provincial Park, Ontario). The publication is named after Sibley Creek
 * on that peninsula; the Giant is the publication's geographic identity.
 *
 * Discipline (Vignelli / canon-reference register):
 *
 *   - SINGLE CONTINUOUS PATH. One M at the start; everything after is C
 *     (cubic bezier). No lifts. The reader's eye traces the silhouette
 *     without breaks - this is the "one line, one idea" move applied to
 *     the brand mark.
 *   - PURE INK stroke (`var(--ink)` = #000000). No fill, no shading, no
 *     texture, no hatching. White paper underneath; Lake Superior is the
 *     implied negative space below the line, sky above.
 *   - Stroke width varies by surface (inline / og), pinned via CSS custom
 *     property `--mark-stroke` so a single component covers all surfaces.
 *   - ONE MTA red dot at the FOOT (rightmost terminus). 3-4px filled
 *     circle. This mirrors the chart canon's latest-print dot: every
 *     visual element on this site ends with one red moment at the right.
 *     ON by default; toggle off only for pure-ink exports.
 *   - Simplified silhouette, NOT topographic. The Giant is geologically a
 *     "formation of mesas and sills" (Wikipedia) - flat-topped diabase
 *     plateaus separated by erosion notches. The mark captures the
 *     multi-mesa structure rather than the single-peak shape the eye
 *     wrongly remembers. Flat tops, not pointed peaks.
 *
 * Orientation (deliberate flip from the photographed view):
 *
 *   In the canonical Thunder Bay photograph the HEAD is on the RIGHT
 *   (since Thunder Bay sits west of the peninsula, looking east; north
 *   is to the viewer's left). For this brand mark we FLIP the view so
 *   HEAD is on the LEFT and FEET are on the RIGHT - the red accent dot
 *   then sits at the rightmost terminus, matching the chart latest-print
 *   dot convention threaded across every visual element on the site.
 *   Recognition of the reclining figure does not depend on cartographic
 *   orientation; brand-system consistency does. Vignelli would do the
 *   same.
 *
 *   The single path runs left-to-right as:
 *
 *     - (4, 72)       left baseline (lake surface, behind the head)
 *     - SHARP CLIFF rise (the 250m southern cliff face of the head, the
 *       most dramatic vertical in the silhouette)
 *     - HEAD MESA plateau, flat-topped, around y=8 from x=18 to x=43
 *       (~25 units of near-horizontal plateau, the tallest single feature)
 *     - THROAT NOTCH bottom around (58, 50) - the DEEPEST notch in the
 *       silhouette; a dramatic plunge from the head plateau down 42 units
 *     - CHIN MESA, smaller flat-topped bump, around y=32 from x=66 to
 *       x=81 (~15 units of subtle plateau)
 *     - ADAM'S APPLE NOTCH bottom around (92, 42) - SHALLOWER than the
 *       throat notch; the chin-to-body transition
 *     - BODY/CHEST MESA, long flat-topped plateau roughly the same
 *       height as the head, around y=12 from x=108 to x=188 (~80 units
 *       of plateau - the longest mesa, the silhouette's visual mass)
 *     - gradual descent off the body's western flank
 *     - subtle KNEE rise around (228, 40)
 *     - continued descent through shin
 *     - FOOT stretch, near-flat at y=58 from roughly x=274 to x=304
 *     - foot terminus at (304, 58) - red dot pinned here
 *
 *   The path terminates at the foot. The red dot sits at that terminus.
 *   The line does NOT continue past the dot down to the lake on the
 *   right - terminating at the foot is the cleaner Vignelli move and
 *   lets the dot read as a terminal stamp (same logic as a chart's
 *   line terminating at its latest-print dot).
 *
 *   Critical recognition rules:
 *
 *     1. THREE distinct flat-topped mesa sections (head, chin, body) -
 *        NOT one peak with undulations. v1 and v2 of this mark both
 *        treated the silhouette as a single dominant peak with a long
 *        cascade; that failed recognition. The Giant is three mesas.
 *     2. TWO notches between the three mesas. The throat notch (between
 *        head and chin) is DEEPER than the Adam's apple notch (between
 *        chin and body).
 *     3. Head and body of COMPARABLE HEIGHT - body is the widest mesa
 *        (the visual mass), head is slightly taller and dramatically
 *        more vertical on its outboard cliff face.
 *     4. FLAT TOPS, not pointed peaks. The mesa plateaus are
 *        near-horizontal at their summits.
 *
 * ViewBox: 320 x 80 (4:1 aspect). Wide landscape. The Giant is a
 * reclining figure read horizontally; the viewBox echoes that.
 *
 * Variants:
 *
 *   variant="inline" (default) - masthead-inline + small surfaces.
 *                                Stroke 1.5px, accent dot 3px.
 *   variant="og"               - OG card hero, 404 page, large-surface
 *                                marks. Stroke 2px, accent dot 4px.
 *
 * Zero client JS. Pure inline SVG, server-rendered.
 */

interface Props {
  /**
   * Pixel width of the rendered mark. Height is derived from the 4:1
   * aspect ratio of the viewBox. Inline-masthead default is ~120px wide
   * (height ~30px); og variant uses ~480-600px wide.
   */
  size?: number;

  /**
   * Surface variant. Affects stroke width and accent-dot scale.
   *   "inline" - 1.5px stroke, 3px dot.
   *   "og"     - 2px stroke, 4px dot.
   */
  variant?: "inline" | "og";

  /**
   * Toggle the MTA red accent dot at the foot. Default true. Set false
   * for a pure-ink export (e.g. a print stationery use, an emboss
   * mock-up, or any surface where the brand has decided to suppress the
   * accent moment).
   */
  withAccent?: boolean;

  /**
   * Optional class hook for surface-specific positioning. The component
   * does not own its parent margins.
   */
  class?: string;
}

const {
  size = 120,
  variant = "inline",
  withAccent = true,
  class: className,
} = Astro.props;

// Variant-driven scales.
const isInline = variant === "inline";
const strokeWidth = isInline ? 1.5 : 2;
const dotR = isInline ? 3 : 4;

// The single continuous path - 320 x 80 viewBox.
// Head on the left, foot on the right (terminus + dot).
// One M, then cubic beziers only. No lifts. ASCII path data.
//
// Multi-mesa structure (corrected v3):
//   - Head mesa top at y=8, plateau from x~18 to x~43
//   - Throat notch bottom at y=50 (deepest)
//   - Chin mesa top at y=32, plateau from x~66 to x~81
//   - Adam's apple notch bottom at y=42 (shallower than throat)
//   - Body mesa top at y=12, long plateau from x~108 to x~188
//   - Knee rise to y=40 around x=228
//   - Foot stretch at y=58 from x~274 to x=304
//
// Bezier discipline for mesa flatness: at each plateau, the segments
// entering and leaving the plateau use control points with the SAME y
// as the plateau endpoints, which produces a near-horizontal tangent
// at the top. At each cliff face, control points are pulled vertically
// (small x delta, large y delta) to produce a near-vertical edge.
const giantPath = [
  // Lake baseline on the left, behind the head's outboard cliff.
  "M 4 72",
  // Sharp cliff rise from the lake to the head plateau (the iconic
  // 250m southern cliff face). Near-vertical: control points hug x=8
  // and x=12 while y plunges from 72 to 8.
  "C 8 60, 12 20, 18 8",
  // Head mesa plateau: flat top from (18,8) to (43,8). Control points
  // share y=8 to enforce a horizontal tangent across the plateau.
  "C 26 8, 35 8, 43 8",
  // Throat notch descent: from the head plateau down to the deepest
  // notch bottom at (58, 50). 42-unit plunge in 15 horizontal units -
  // the silhouette's most dramatic single feature.
  "C 50 8, 54 36, 58 50",
  // Rise out of the throat notch onto the chin mesa at (66, 32).
  "C 60 50, 63 36, 66 32",
  // Chin mesa plateau: short flat top from (66,32) to (81,32).
  "C 72 32, 76 32, 81 32",
  // Adam's apple notch descent to (92, 42) - SHALLOWER than throat.
  "C 85 32, 89 40, 92 42",
  // Rise out of Adam's apple notch onto the body plateau at (108, 12).
  // Body height (12) is just below head height (8) - comparable mass.
  "C 96 42, 102 18, 108 12",
  // Body mesa plateau, long flat top from (108,12) to (188,12). This
  // is the longest mesa - the silhouette's visual mass. Control points
  // share y=12 to enforce a horizontal tangent across the full plateau.
  "C 135 12, 162 12, 188 12",
  // Gradual descent off the body's western flank into the knees.
  "C 204 14, 218 28, 228 40",
  // Subtle knee rise / plateau - small undulation, not a peak.
  "C 238 42, 248 44, 258 48",
  // Shin descent toward the feet.
  "C 266 52, 270 56, 274 58",
  // Foot stretch, near-flat at y=58 to the terminus at (304, 58).
  "C 284 58, 294 58, 304 58",
].join(" ");

// Foot-terminus anchor for the red dot. Matches the last C endpoint.
const dotCX = 304;
const dotCY = 58;
---

<svg
  class:list={["sg-mark", `sg-mark--${variant}`, className]}
  width={size}
  height={size / 4}
  viewBox="0 0 320 80"
  preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
  role="img"
  aria-label="Sibley Creek mark: stylized silhouette of the Sleeping Giant on the Sibley Peninsula, Lake Superior"
  style={`--mark-stroke: ${strokeWidth}px;`}
>
  <title>Sibley Creek - the Sleeping Giant, Sibley Peninsula, Lake Superior</title>
  <path class="sg-mark__line" d={giantPath} />
  {withAccent && (
    <circle class="sg-mark__dot" cx={dotCX} cy={dotCY} r={dotR} />
  )}
</svg>

<style>
  /*
   * The mark sits on paper. Container ships no fill, no border, no
   * background. Negative-space rules live in the consuming surface
   * (the masthead, the OG card frame, etc.) so the mark itself is
   * portable.
   */
  .sg-mark {
    display: inline-block;
    vertical-align: middle;
  }

  .sg-mark__line {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--ink);
    stroke-width: var(--mark-stroke, 1.5px);
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    /* Keep the line at its specified stroke width regardless of the
     * wrapper's CSS-driven scale. Same rationale as the canon-reference
     * chart line. */
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  .sg-mark__dot {
    fill: var(--accent);
    /* No stroke ring. The canon's brand-signal dot is a filled circle
     * on the paper, not a ringed marker - matches the latest-print dot
     * convention on charts (canon-compliance Rule 2). */
    stroke: none;
  }
</style>
